      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1765108737192,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Proposed Solutions for the NFR deep dives ",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Proposed Solutions for the NFR deep dives ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "bPc6MyMQc4M7trwOtrXxQ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1527.1063399637405,
      "y": 554.535498104699,
      "width": 732.2638363039475,
      "height": 675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0x",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1319876784,
      "version": 5401,
      "versionNonce": 1153121664,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1765108737192,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Consistency for swipes: \n    - If we are using NoSql => eventual consistency. Now what we could do\nhere is run a cron job that looks for entries for match in the db every\nhour or something. The end user won't know when it happened. \n    - Another option is to use a cache with certain TTL such that during\nthis time our entries will be there in the DB for eventual consistency. In\nthe cache we store key: user1-user2 => value: Yes|No \nNow when the other user swipes right we check in the cache for the key if\nthere then its a match. if not there then we check in the database and\nconfirm. \n\n2) Low Latency for profile stack: \n    - One way is to do geo-Sharding\n        - issues: what if user change their location\n        - some locations are more densely populated\n    - Another solution could be to recomputing the stack and storing in a\ncache ready to go. \n        - issues: Once the stack is gone trough then again calculation time \n        - If the user updates the preference(location, age ...) post the job\nis completed then its a redundant data shown to user \n        - geospatial Index \n        - Using search optimised database: Elastic Search, geo indexing can\nbe done here. 3\n        - further we can combine above to approaches. the cron job &\nelastic \n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Consistency for swipes: \n    - If we are using NoSql => eventual consistency. Now what we could do here is run a cron job that looks for entries for match in the db every hour or something. The end user won't know when it happened. \n    - Another option is to use a cache with certain TTL such that during this time our entries will be there in the DB for eventual consistency. In the cache we store key: user1-user2 => value: Yes|No \nNow when the other user swipes right we check in the cache for the key if there then its a match. if not there then we check in the database and confirm. \n\n2) Low Latency for profile stack: \n    - One way is to do geo-Sharding\n        - issues: what if user change their location\n        - some locations are more densely populated\n    - Another solution could be to recomputing the stack and storing in a cache ready to go. \n        - issues: Once the stack is gone trough then again calculation time \n        - If the user updates the preference(location, age ...) post the job is completed then its a redundant data shown to user \n        - geospatial Index \n        - Using search optimised database: Elastic Search, geo indexing can be done here. 3\n        - further we can combine above to approaches. the cron job & elastic \n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
